# Layout 3 — Three-pane workspace (versions left, content center, graph right)

Version timeline becomes a first-class left rail (switch version = switch view). Right pane is the relation graph neighborhood.

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Tools / Hammer drill                                         [Publish] [⋮]       │
├───────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────┤
│ VERSIONS      │  Details                                │ Connected              │
│               │  ───────────────────────────            │  → kit of              │
│ ● v3 Draft ←  │  Name      Hammer drill                 │    • Site kit A        │
│ ○ v2 Latest   │  Power     1100 W                       │  ← stored in           │
│ ○ v1          │  Weight    2.9 kg                       │    • Depot 7 [v2]      │
│ ○ v0 Archived │                                         │  ← used by             │
│               │  Labels                                 │    • Crew North        │
│ [New version] │  ◉ equipment ◉ heavy ◉ rental [+]       │                        │
│ [Discard]     │                                         │  [+ connect]           │
│               │  ┌ Notes ── Tasks ── Activity ──────┐   │                        │
│ viewing v3    │  │ ▸ ☐ Order spare batteries        │   │                        │
│ (draft)       │  │ ▸ "Check chuck wear" — Kalle     │   │                        │
│               │  └──────────────────────────────────┘   │                        │
└───────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────┘
```

**Pros:** version switching is one click + always shows what you're viewing; relations get a dedicated full-height pane.
**Cons:** heavy chrome for non-versioned concepts (left rail collapses away → two layouts to maintain); center column narrow on laptops.
**Best when:** versioning is the core workflow and users compare/flip versions a lot.
